#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b6dd2970-3cdc-58fc-8f28-0bfdcd738d6f" :question/math-academy-id "e-593" :question/problem "The region below enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt x$ and $y=x$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $y=1$. Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/36/362a92640ed68dfd5fa85f0338d95233003e9f8a1b059a5dffa2cda243a1411b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x & =\\sqrt x \\\\[3pt] x-\\sqrt x & = 0 \\\\[3pt] \\sqrt x \\left(\\sqrt x - 1\\right) &= 0 \\\\[3pt] x& = 0, 1 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/5456bed18c03e823c8e9388571b5671f5f476f5caa1085793314b57080b2b07c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=x$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=x$.![](images/51/5136903876ed5ea811ebf28e0da905b5029b2c9311527fce9d0fed8e7bd1dd46.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(1) - (x)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[1 - x]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=\\sqrt{x}$.![](images/c8/c850643bb1c315d0516eff55d6270078504c1c98687cfac40e0c9b4b2c771b7c.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{inner}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[(1) - (\\sqrt{x})]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[1 - \\sqrt{x}]^{2}dx$.\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$\\begin{aligned} V &= {\\color{blue}V_{\\text{outer}}} - {\\color{red}V_{\\text{inner}}} \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 [1-x]^2 - \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 [1-\\sqrt x]^2\\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\left((1-x)^2 - (1-\\sqrt x)^2\\right) \\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2-3x+2\\sqrt x) \\,\\,\\textrm d x \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi\\left. \\left(\\dfrac {1} 3 x^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} x^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3} x^{3/2} \\ \\right)\\right|_0^1 \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\left[\\left(\\dfrac {1}3 (1)^3 - \\dfrac{3}{2}(1)^2 + \\dfrac{4}{3}(1)^{3/2} \\right) - (0) \\right] \\\\[5pt] & = \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}3 - \\dfrac{3}{2} +\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {\\pi}{6}. \\end{aligned}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b79b0d88-0b42-5223-927b-5a4023f6c329" :question/math-academy-id "e-594" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curves $x=y^2$ and $x=2-y^2$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the line $x=-1$. Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/e8/e8f0f999521058f3213e11bd3df67a95a6ea8604e6853d06923ffbba6cf9d17c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, to determine the limits of integration, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 2 - y^{2} \\\\\n2y^{2} &= 2 \\\\\ny^{2} &= 1 \\\\\ny &= ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1$.\n\nNext, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/c3/c3c9217600a26e11614752b2ca2ac8b3aa6c12b0df9f3a8c13d2b32c5b338107.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y^2$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x={2-y^2}$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of outer and inner solids.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={2-y^2}$.![](images/36/3696bac064f5157b5c4ab3aefe34128d2d126a0a6b586106eb1e439bc90e7df5.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{outer}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(2 - y^{2}) - (-1)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[3 - y^{2}]^{2}dy$.\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y^2$.![](images/f5/f59ee5966f0cb9572d00e7f7278a7462841aef6b18f4865924303ad65b50738c.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{inner}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[(y^{2}) - (-1)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[y^{2} + 1]^{2}dy$.\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[3 - y^{2}]^{2}dx - π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}[y^{2} + 1]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}([3 - y^{2}]^{2} - [y^{2} + 1]^{2})dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}(8 - 8y^{2})dy; = \\left.π(8y - \\frac{8y^{3}}{3})\\right|_{-1}^{1}; = π[(8 - \\frac{8}{3}) - (-8 + \\frac{8}{3})]; = π(\\frac{32}{3}); = \\frac{32π}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096569 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9016e61-3365-5f53-ae10-cc94e9c638f3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "8e436e4c-45da-528c-885b-9a094919a652" :question/math-academy-id "e-7508" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8a9dd6c0bd31ceb221d0d72e6f0238ab51760cd9a32ba92bb8bdc85429a899f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our vertical arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/59/59041e6817cbf26f9de00de0ec864cea57f1a419877fe5c3203857c5488c7324.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $y=-2x$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curver $y=2x^2$.\n\nNow, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $y=2x^2$.![](images/07/0761073a5f7f417a07f35cb0b7c08dd0b4ea6bd84da71f3e474911ee036c546b.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(3) - (2x^{2})]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[3 - 2x^{2}]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $y=-2x$.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea5e5dd50dcd623532557e5365118ae5c9b950be059ba8c0069c7b36071f2c3.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{inner} &= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(upper) - (lower)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[(3) - (-2x)]^{2}dx \\\\\n&= π\\int_{-1}^{0}[3 + 2x]^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}[3 - 2x^{2}]^{2}dx - π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}[3 + 2x]^{2}dx; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{0}([3 - 2x^{2}]^{2} - [3 + 2x]^{2})dx$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26659894-bc6d-50fe-a5f8-029c962452cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d064d066-3bbe-5381-ab56-3fc407da144f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7509" :question/problem "The region enclosed by the curves $x=-y^2$ and $x = -y$ is rotated $360^\\circ$ about the line $x={1}$. Find an integral that gives the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n![](images/64/6471c937fb79a2266188668ebdb698eb926655b6002f8f259410b96efcb15247.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-y^{2} &= -y \\\\\ny^{2} - y &= 0 \\\\\ny(y - 1) &= 0 \\\\\ny &= 0, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, our limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/4d/4da6108728a226e19cea708a09381c9054b129590d2a6d2c07fbfacdc30367a5.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=-{y}^2$, and\n- *leaves* though the outer curve $x=-y$.\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x=-y$.![](images/02/02798e08e1eb8cae41e4754cfed267401298d454ce2ef856f47f2ae87a8543ee.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{outer} &= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[(1) - (-y)]^{2}dy \\\\\n&= π\\int_{0}^{1}[1 + y]^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=-{y}^2$.![](images/5c/5c5e2060b1321856349719987e43b3424d49b465255295b8777002d85cbe6792.png)\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n$V_{inner}|= π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[(right) - (left)]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[(1) - (-y^{2})]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[1 + y^{2}]^{2}dy$.\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.Finally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n$V|= V_{outer} - V_{inner}; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[1 + y]^{2}dy - π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[1 + y^{2}]^{2}dy; = π\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}([1 + y]^{2} - [1 + y^{2}]^{2})dy$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096730 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c6aa618-4d2f-5ce2-9bb2-c6b528ec9b7b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff877dd-2549-5c21-9e2a-6532f961d06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095184} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f4e718-9113-5e14-b3a1-0832603fb983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095185} {:answer/id #uuid "01ca3682-a08d-5f29-b1dd-5cc052a3dd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095186} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb7b310-6130-5147-9dc4-46a2a12b4e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{63π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095187} {:answer/id #uuid "77ca701e-f1df-516f-9397-34fd75769c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095188}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5fce8d-1e83-5220-b88b-1bc28cf4c002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095183}] :question/id #uuid "89556ba4-a6e5-533a-b6c1-5971b5251496" :question/math-academy-id "q-30118" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c62793a0dc7ff37a4d6b7cf157239f21e6b0cdef8c3d428916f074584462598b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $y = 3x$ for $y ∈ [0, 9]$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the line $x = 5$. Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096729 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2e0268f-36a5-5f55-a0ae-9e0f34981dab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About a Horizontal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917fd200-4825-5986-a9cf-3c79e16e4efd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095304} {:answer/id #uuid "b0550a0f-dd07-59ef-815f-5deaf309a550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095305} {:answer/id #uuid "133980ed-4bc8-5ef7-91f2-8bd82cd6f8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11π}{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095306} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7c9518-63b1-57ba-a0bb-48f90369d7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14π}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095307} {:answer/id #uuid "52dcde8b-1e0a-5309-b056-7ac113f5aaa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3π}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095308}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b658f2-ac3d-5bc5-8869-0fed4b76530f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095303}] :question/id #uuid "5ae9fb77-cc16-5042-8460-37410a7d25d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-3385" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8d2665ab162ff21e358a80a6e42659b0385244a08d94f8de3ad5df12b434a9a.png)\n\nThe region above enclosed by the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $y = \\sqrt{x}$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the line $y = 1$. Find the volume of the resulting solid.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
